{"type": "thinking", "text": "Even when in Amaze, the graph icon opens the same volume/root properties (Name \"0\", location /storage/emulated). So it's the storage root properties, not current folder. Good.\n\nThis dialog's \"Name: 0\" - if it's the storage volume properties, wrong. If it's the folder /storage/emulated/0 properties, correct-ish. The graph icon is in the storage row of the drawer; the drawer's row displays \"Internal Storage\". So likely volume properties. The Name \"0\" is a clear mismatch with the drawer's \"Internal Storage\". Also \"Location /storage/emulated\" vs \"Internal Storage\" root /storage/emulated/0? Hmm.\n\nActually, maybe the graph icon opens properties of the *storage volume's root directory* /storage/emulated/0, which is a folder named \"0\" inside /storage/emulated. The dialog's Name \"0\" would be the folder name. The \"Location\" /storage/emulated is its parent. That's exactly what a folder-properties dialog would show. The \"Size: 16 items; 369 GB\" would be the folder's properties. The chart's legend \"used space\" vs \"size of folder\" maybe because the volume is the root? Hmm.\n\nIf it's folder properties, the item count 16 and size 369 GB are the folder's stats:\n- In the file list, /storage/emulated/0 shows \"14 folders and 0 files\" = 14 items.\n- The properties dialog shows \"16 items\" — 2 more. Why? Possibly counts hidden files (.trashed? 2 hidden folders) while the list hides them. Hmm. If hidden files were visible at launch, root had 28 folders, not 16. So 16 doesn't match 28 or 14. Odd.\n- The size 369 GB = free space, definitely not the folder's data size (which should be ~82.54 GB used).\n\nThis strongly suggests the folder-size background computation for the storage root returns free space instead of content size. That's the properties bug! And the item count 16 might be unrelated/wrong too.\n\nBut how to know the correct folder size? The chart shows \"used space 82.54 GB\" which likely equals the sum of all data on the volume (including /storage/emulated/0). Since the folder /storage/emulated/0 contains all user data, its size should be ~82.54 GB (or less due to system). The dialog's own chart says 82.54 GB used. So Size should be ~82.54 GB, not 369 GB. This is a self-contradictory dialog. Great.\n\nAlso the properties dialog for normal folders shows \"size of folder\" red segment plus \"used by others\" and \"free space\". For the root folder, the chart shows \"used space\" = 82.54 GB (which includes the folder's data). The red segment corresponds to the whole volume's used space, not the folder. This dialog seems to be *volume properties* rather than folder properties: it knows total 452, free 369, used 82.54. The \"Size\" line \"16 items; 369 GB\" might actually be `16 items; 369 GB` where 369 GB is the *free* space intentionally? If it's volume properties, \"Size\" should be 452 GB.\n\nEither way, the \"Size\" line is wrong: it equals free space, not the volume total or content size, and the same dialog shows Total 452 GB. I'm fairly confident this is a bug. It's likely the drawer storage list bug (volume info) or properties bug.\n\nLet's file it. Use element_ref 6, observed_quote \"16 items; 369 GB\". Screen: Properties dialog for Internal Storage opened from the drawer storage row's usage graph. Expected: The \"Size\" line should reflect the storage's total (452 GB) / used content (82.54 GB) consistent with the same dialog's chart, not the free space. Honestly, for volume properties, \"Size\" should be \"452 GB\". Let me phrase expected: \"Size should show the storage volume's total size 452 GB (as the dialog's chart shows Total: 452 GB); at minimum it must not show the free-space figure 369 GB.\"\n\nHmm, but if it's folder properties, expected is 82.54 GB. To avoid being wrong, I can emphasize the internal contradiction and not prescribe the exact number? The tool requires an \"expected\" string; I can say \"The Size value must agree with the rest of the dialog (Total 452 GB, used 82.54 GB, free 369 GB); it currently repeats the free-space value 369 GB.\" That's acceptable.\n\nAlso mention Name \"0\" vs drawer \"Internal Storage\"? That's a separate issue; I could include in observed but not expected. Maybe make a second report? Let's first file Size.\n\nActually, maybe the \"Name 0\" is arguably correct folder name. Focus on Size.\n\nLet's file."}
